WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:02.420
Mysterious Andorians?

00:00:02.420 --> 00:00:04.430
A reference to unrequited love?

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:07.009
A return to a familiar star system?

00:00:07.009 --> 00:00:11.934
Here are all of the Easter eggs you missed
in "Star Trek: Strange New Worlds" episode two.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:17.180
The series' second episode "Children of the
Comet" begins with a log entry from Cadet Uhura.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.410
She is still getting used to life aboard the
Enterprise and is about to attend a dinner

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:23.160
in the Captain's quarters.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:27.810
There she meets the ship's new chief engineer,
an Andorian named Hemmer.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:32.890
But Hemmer is no ordinary Andorian, the normally
blue-skinned, antennaed aliens who debuted

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:35.230
in the original series episode "Journey to
Babel."

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:40.390
Hemmer is a subspecies of Andorian known as
Aenar, who live in the frozen wastelands on

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:45.491
Andoria, as seen in the "Enterprise" fourth
season episode appropriately titled, "The Aenar."

00:00:45.491 --> 00:00:50.320
When Uhura spots Hemmer chopping vegetables
she offers to assist, knowing he is visually

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.559
impaired, as Aenar are all blind.

00:00:52.559 --> 00:00:56.399
Spock points out that Hemmer's other senses
compensate for his blindness, to which the

00:00:56.399 --> 00:00:57.770
Aenar responds:

00:00:57.770 --> 00:00:58.770
"Compensate?

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:01.079
They are superior."

00:01:01.079 --> 00:01:05.800
It's also mentioned that the Aenar have a
form of precognition, and Spock helps demonstrate

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:07.520
Hemmer's telepathic abilities.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:12.000
Both of these unique abilities were showcased
in the "Enterprise" three-part story that

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.200
introduced a young Aenar woman named Jhamel
and her brother Gareb, who until now were

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.830
the only Aenar ever seen on "Star Trek."

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:24.630
In that story, the Romulans kidnapped Gareb
and used his abilities to pilot a deadly new

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:29.050
drone ship in an attempt to provoke a war
between galactic powers.

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:33.640
During his dinner with the crew, Captain Pike
entertains with a story from his earlier years.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:38.310
He was chasing down a pantsless Nausicaan
while working a security job and fell flat

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:43.030
on his face after tripping over the alien's
pants, phaser still in hand.

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:47.060
It's an incident that would apparently play
at least some role in his switch to command,

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:52.780
but it wasn't the last time a future Enterprise
captain would tangle with some nasty Nausicaans.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:57.620
While we haven't seen Nausicaans that often,
this brutish alien race made a memorable first

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:00.550
impression in the "Next Generation" episode
"Tapestry."

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:04.160
In that story, Picard dies and meets Q in
the afterlife.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:07.570
He offers him a chance to go back in time
and change his past.

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:12.610
In doing so, Picard revisits a violent incident
from his early days in Starfleet.

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:17.000
While awaiting his first orders after graduating
from Starfleet Academy, Picard and two friends

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.960
have a run-in with some Nausicaans who attempt
to cheat them in a game of dom-jot.

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:25.490
Defending his friend from a vicious melee,
Picard is impaled through the back by one

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:30.230
of the aliens, requiring him to have an artificial
heart implanted that he'd carry for the rest

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:31.680
of his natural life.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:36.590
Thankfully, it doesn't sound like Pike's brush
with Nausicaans was quite so harrowing.

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:41.077
The second episode of "Strange New Worlds"
takes the Enterprise to the Persephone star system.

00:02:41.077 --> 00:02:45.420
A planet there is endangered by a massive
comet that is due to strike its surface in

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:47.420
a world-ending event.

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:51.910
In his briefing, Spock says that the people
on the planet are a primitive race with no

00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:55.280
way to know the threat they face and no way
to stop it.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.620
With the people below unable to save themselves,
Pike and the crew get to work on finding a

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:02.400
way to divert the comet from Persephone III.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:06.540
But this won't be the last time an Enterprise
crew makes a visit to Persephone.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:10.380
It originally popped up the first season of
"Star Trek: The Next Generation," set nearly

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:11.760
a century later.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:16.560
Though it wasn't Persephone III, the Enterprise
D visited the Persephone system briefly in

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:18.600
the episode "Too Short a Season."

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:23.700
In that story, Picard and his Enterprise crew
travel to Persephone V to pick up a renowned

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:27.450
Federation negotiator, the elderly Admiral
Mark Jameson.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:31.569
From there, they warp to Mordan IV to oversee
the release of prisoners taken in a terrorist

00:03:31.569 --> 00:03:32.950
hostage situation.

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:34.830
Things don't go as planned, of course.

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:39.280
It's revealed that Jameson himself may be
responsible for the crisis, the fallout from

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:42.900
another incident he was involved in decades
before.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:47.890
Nurse Christine Chapel and Mr. Spock had a
brief interaction in the premiere episode,

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:52.260
and their encounter in "Children of the Comet"
made it clear there's some serious tension

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:55.220
there — at least from Chapel's side of things.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:04:00.849
As Spock, La'an, Uhura, and Lt. Sam Kirk prepare
to beam to the surface of the comet, Chapel

00:04:00.849 --> 00:04:03.120
inoculates them with an anti-radiation drug.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:08.170
When she administers a dose to Spock, she
openly flirts with the Vulcan science officer,

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:10.060
though Spock doesn't seem to notice.

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:14.630
Uhura does, however, and mocks him later in
the episode, sarcastically referring to Chapel

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:15.810
as his girlfriend.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:17.269
"I am a Vulcan.

00:04:17.269 --> 00:04:19.019
We are too honest by nature."

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:22.250
"Your girlfriend must love that."

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:27.370
The sparks are, of course, a direct reference
to the original series, in which Nurse Chapel,

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:31.470
played then by series creator Gene Roddenberry's
wife Majel Barrett, showed an open attraction

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:34.990
to Spock and even got jealous of his relationship
with T'Pring.

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:39.160
It was in the episode "The Naked Time" that
Chapel professed her love for him, and in

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:41.560
"Plato's Stepchildren" they even shared a
kiss.

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:46.240
Though in the episode it was while they were
coerced by alien telepaths, and neither was

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:48.300
entirely comfortable with the encounter.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:53.070
Under the influence of a love potion, Spock
himself became infatuated with Chapel in the

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:57.220
quasi-canonical "Star Trek: The Animated Series"
episode "Mudd's Passion."

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:02.370
Where "Strange New Worlds" will take their
relationship remains to be seen.

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:06.520
Inside the alien structure on the comet's
surface, the landing party discovers a mysterious

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:07.880
alien egg.

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:11.520
Unable to get many readings, it's up to Uhura
to decipher the inscriptions on the egg's

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:14.820
surface and find a way to lower the comet's
artificial shielding.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:18.500
If she can't, the comet will destroy Persephone
III.

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:22.680
Uhura unwittingly discovers how to communicate
with the egg when she begins humming an old

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:25.440
Kenyan folk melody to alleviate her anxiety.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:29.389
As it turns out, the comet's intelligence
communicates through musical tones.

00:05:29.389 --> 00:05:34.990
Diehard Trekkies will recall that in the original
series, Uhura had shown an aptitude for singing,

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:37.639
and was seen doing so on more than one occasion.

00:05:37.639 --> 00:05:42.090
She was most fond of a song called "Beyond
Antares," which she sang in two different

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:45.110
episodes, "The Conscience of the King" and
"The Changeling."

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:49.210
Later in "Children of the Comet," Uhura and
Spock sing together to communicate with the

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:53.460
intelligence, and, as "Star Trek" fans know,
this wouldn't be the last time they perform

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:54.740
side by side.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:06:00.020
In the 1966 episode "Charlie X," Spock plays
a Vulcan lute while Uhura accompanies him

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:05.830
with her sweet voice, performing a song titled
"Oh, On the Starship Enterprise."

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:09.680
As the landing party works inside the comet
to alter its course, Pike and the Enterprise

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:12.260
find themselves confronted by an alien ship.

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:15.520
The beings aboard it refer to themselves as
"The Shepherds."

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:21.110
They warn Pike not to interfere with the comet,
which they believe is a kind of divine life-bringer.

00:06:21.110 --> 00:06:26.180
This is a powerful starship that the Enterprise
can't compete with, so they're careful not

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:29.960
to anger them, but they cannot abandon the
mission to save the planet either.

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:34.622
When they're finally forced into a firefight,
Pike issues a command to get them out of there, barking

00:06:34.622 --> 00:06:36.979
"Escape pattern April Omega Three!"

00:06:36.979 --> 00:06:41.199
This is a clear reference to Robert April,
the former Enterprise captain who we met in

00:06:41.199 --> 00:06:42.889
the "Strange New Worlds" premiere.

00:06:42.889 --> 00:06:46.790
April became official canon in an episode
of "Star Trek: Discovery," where his name

00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:50.949
appeared on a computer screen as part of a
list of Starfleet's most decorated captains,

00:06:50.949 --> 00:06:55.919
alongside Pike, Jonathan Archer, Matthew Decker
from the original series episode "The Doomsday

00:06:55.919 --> 00:06:57.800
Machine," and Philippa Georgiou.

00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:02.040
Given that April has an escape maneuver named
after him, it's no wonder he's considered

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:03.669
one of the best.

